it's a question that's puzzled  filmmakers for generations how do you  show a cinematic divine being from Star  Trek 5 excuse me I'd just like to ask a  question what does God need with the  starship to Clash of the Titans the  movies have wrestled with how to show  God and gods on a big screen and the  hottest ticket in Hollywood the Marvel  Cinematic Universe is no exception to  this struggle Marvel Studios has already  made multiple feature film starring  members belonging to the pantheon of  Norse gods Charlie when former head of  visual development at Marvel Studios led  his team to design the cinematic  versions of Thor and his Asgardian pals  we sat down with when for an exclusive  interview and he explained that two very  important members of Thor's immediate  family almost looked very very different  Odin and Loki Thor's dad and adopted  brother respectively were first drawn in  Marvel Comics by the legendary Jack  Kirby who probably didn't draw many or  any actual mythological details to bring  them to the page both debuted shortly  after Thor's own first comic book  appearance in journey into mystery  number 83 in 1962 but even though they  both showed up right around the same  time in the funny books there looks  couldn't have been more different in  terms of design and even simple  consistency Loki's costume from journey  into mystery number 85 may not be a  pretty sight but it is an excellent  example of how to design a bad guy where  Thor wears blue red and has graceful  looking wings on his helmet Loki is a  perfect contrast to those details his  outfit consists of hues from the other  side of the color wheel green and yellow  and instead of a headpiece with wings  that angle backward his own outfit  features aggressive horns that jut out  word while Loki's look has evolved over  the years these have been constant  features that appear in nearly every  incarnation Odin on the other hand  couldn't be less consistent or defined  Odin's first appearance sporting a style  best described as comic-book Viking  stuff only reveals his head appearing in  the clouds over Thor Mufasa style  that what you have become you must take  your place in the circle of life his  next batch of appearances are no clearer  with various designs that include  assortments of helmets capes more  helmets furs and what sure seems to be a  cummerbund with a lightning bolt hastily  drawn on it it's a lightning bolt with  extra powers considering how important  and powerful  Odin is supposed to be he might want to  try getting dressed in front of a mirror  for once and maybe fire whatever  crappier Norse gods have the job of  dressing him the all father's look  continued to evolve each time he  appeared making it seem like artists  relish the opportunity to use him for  experiments new space Viking designs  even when writer artist Walt Simonson  took control of steering the Asgardian  Royal Family's adventures with Thor  number 337 Odin never stayed in one  outfit for long  Simonson's run is famous for introducing  beta ray bill frog Thor and that  incredible scene when scourge machine  guns a bunch of demons but as Odin  remains as impossible to pin down as  anyone elses when Charlie when became  the head of visual development at Marvel  Studios one of his first tasks was  adapting the mythical land of Asgard and  it's powerful inhabitants for the first  Thor movie which arrived in 2011 and  that meant trying to define the looks  for Thor's immediate family members but  before he could settle on a specific  direction when first had to find  directions he wanted to avoid that's why  he came up with this version of Thor's  villainous brother which when calls  loony Loki while loony Loki retains the  classic green and gold colors from the  comics nearly every other element seems  transformed when explained  Loki's horns are you know it's really  represented in this floating shape  that's behind him that also changes will  float back up into shapes that you'll  recognize as being his horns this is  more like how do we still capture that  character but in unique ways this  version of the character was never  really meant to show up on film  in fact this design came about before  there was a finished script and before  director Kenneth Branagh came on to  direct the project rather wen was  experimenting seeing how far out of  shape Loki could be bent before he  became unrecognizable here I was trying  to push it as far as I could  so as my god I understood the icon let  me try to  rip it as far apart from that while  still trying to maintain who that is it  probably broke at some point but that's  actually what you're trying to do is  like understand how far do you go until  it actually breaks and this is one of  those explorations of like I need to  push this when I show that to people  that are just big low-key fans they're  just like terror I'm so glad that it  wasn't like that but I mean we needed to  go there Loki wasn't the only character  to get stretched past the breaking point  because of Odin's ever-changing fashion  there's not much when could have done in  his early designs that would have been  too out of bounds it's fascinating to  imagine the Asgard when was building  early on since it seems like it would  have gone more into Thor's sci-fi  elements than the Shakespearean super  fantasy we got instead and on that note  this concept art of Odin is outfit and  its choice of furniture speaks volumes  about the movie that could have been the  MCU is filled with magic but that magic  is really science that we don't  understand right and so that was the  premise that was set up both on Iron Man  and on to Thor right so Thor's is a  magical world this realm that we didn't  understand but we wanted to still be  based in science let's look at odhh ins  chair which consists of floating  platforms that seemed to have eventually  appeared in the first guardians of the  galaxy movie for thanasis throne when 